WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.458
MARK MERCHANDANI:
We're talking all

00:00:01.458 --> 00:00:03.690
about Kubernetes-- coding,
Enterprise functionality,

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:04.642
and serverless.

00:00:04.642 --> 00:00:06.600
Welcome to "This Week in
Cloud," where we serve

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.282
you the lowest latency news.

00:00:08.282 --> 00:00:09.990
We talked about some
of the announcements

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:10.870
at Google Cloud Next.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.960
But there was so
much to show, there's

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.305
no way we could fit it
into just one video.

00:00:15.305 --> 00:00:17.430
We've compiled a huge list
of announcements for you

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.972
to check out and see which ones
you can use to start building

00:00:19.972 --> 00:00:20.628
with today.

00:00:20.628 --> 00:00:22.170
Speaking of which,
here are some ones

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:23.870
that we're really excited about.

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:25.620
First up is one that
you may have missed--

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:26.880
Cloud Code.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.800
Cloud Code is a set of
plugins for Intelligent

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.960
and Visual Studio Code
that brings automation

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.420
and assistance to every phase
of the software development

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.820
lifecycle, from local
developer environments

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.110
right to the cloud.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.970
And with integrated
command line tools,

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.832
like Scaffold, Jib, and
kubectl under the hood,

00:00:41.832 --> 00:00:43.290
Cloud Code gives
you everything you

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.840
need for developing with
Kubernetes and the managed

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.190
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:48.325
Give it a try.

00:00:48.325 --> 00:00:49.950
Speaking of managed
Kubernetes, did you

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.133
catch the GKE
Advanced announcement?

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:53.800
We've just published
the first blog post

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.880
in a series that talks about
what GKE Advanced offers.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.680
Think of it as the GKE
you're already familiar with,

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:02.190
but with a bunch of
Enterprise-requested features,

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.769
such as automatic
vertical pod auto-scaling

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:06.930
and node auto-provisioning.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.083
More integrations
and an enhanced SLA

00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:10.500
give you the
flexibility to choose

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.240
the right level of
managed Kubernetes

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.270
and to launch your
application on Google Cloud.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:17.603
Now, if you're using
Kubernetes to help orchestrate

00:01:17.603 --> 00:01:19.020
but you still want
the [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.260
to utilize serverless,
Knative is worth checking out.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.425
Google Developer
Advocate Mete Atamel

00:01:24.425 --> 00:01:26.550
has a GitHub repo that
walks you through setting up

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.400
GKE with Istio and
Knative, then covers

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.620
the three main components--
serving, eventing,

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:32.770
and building.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.280
This is a great way to start
exploring how serverless

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.680
can help run your application
without worrying about how

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.300
the underlying logistics work.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.257
And best of all-- you get some
hands-on Knative experience.

00:01:42.257 --> 00:01:44.340
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.350
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:47.520
Have any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.390
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:51.740
We'll see you in the cloud.

